{"episodes":{"199513":"A broken line of thunderstorms advanced across Middle Tennessee during the evening of March 19, 2025, ahead of a cold front. The main threat with these thunderstorms was strong winds with numerous reports of downed trees received, especially in and around the Nashville metro area.","199880":"A low pressure system brought thunderstorms to central Indiana during the evening of March 19. The storms produced an EF-2 tornado to Bartholomew County along with wind damage and large hail to much of central Indiana.","200195":"A low pressure system moved through the region, producing numerous storms.","200205":"A low pressure system moved through the region, producing numerous storms.","200216":"A low pressure system moved through the region, producing numerous storms.","200274":"A strong low pressure system tracked towards central Illinois on the afternoon of Wednesday, March 19. Strong gradient winds produced frequent gusts of 40-55 mph across the region, but no damage was noted from these winds. Clusters of thunderstorms developed across the warm sector during the afternoon and continued into the evening, resulting in two tornadoes in the NWS Lincoln County Warning Area, along with numerous reports of wind damage and scattered hail. The tornadoes occurred in Tazewell County (EF-U) and Coles County (EF-1). Thunderstorm winds resulted in one injury in McLean County when a semi-truck was overturned on I-39. Several gustnadoes were reported during this event, as thunderstorm winds kicked up dust across unplanted fields. Additional storms developed along a dry line during the evening, producing sporadic severe wind gusts and small hail.","200316":"An upper-level trough digging into the plains on the morning of the 19th resulted in broad diffluent level flow over the lower Ohio Valley. A surface low moving from Kansas into northern Missouri in the afternoon brought continued strong southerly flow at the surface. Although instability was modest, 500-1000J/kg, 80-90kts of deep layer shear and plenty of low-level shear set the stage for a minor severe thunderstorm event in the early evening. Scattered thunderstorms developed over southern Illinois in the late afternoon and eventually formed into a line as they moved east across west Kentucky through the  evening. The storms produced sporadic reports of quarter size hail along and east of the Pennyrile Parkway/Interstate 69 corridor. A 65 mph wind gust was measured on the west side of Hopkinsville and a few trees were blown down through town.","200335":"A broken line of storms along a cold front moved through on the evening of March 19 resulting in a few instances of tree and property damage along with two EF-0 tornadoes.","200342":"A broken line of storms along a cold front moved through on the evening of March 19 resulting in a few instances of tree and property damage.","200475":"A midlevel trough and an associated surface cold front progressed from the Appalachians into the Atlantic coast on March 20. This allowed for an isolated severe thunderstorm to form across central Virginia.","200934":"As a low pressure system slid from the Ozarks towards the Great Lakes, a north to south oriented cold front moved east through the Lower Ohio Valley. A line of convection moving ahead of the front saw some stronger embedded cells develop. These cells produced damaging winds and severe hail over parts of southern Indiana.","200942":"As a low pressure system slid from the Ozarks towards the Great Lakes, a north to south oriented cold front moved east through the Lower Ohio Valley. A line of convection moving ahead of the front saw some stronger embedded cells develop. These cells produced damaging winds and severe hail over parts of north central Kentucky.","201296":"Scattered thunderstorms ahead of a cold front produced gusty winds as they moved over the Chesapeake Bay on March 20."},"events":[{"begin":"2025-03-20T00:04Z","county":"Lawrence","deaths":0,"end":"2025-03-20T00:04Z","